  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "One server",
      "title": "It starts with one server",
      "chapter": "setup",
      "body": "Your app runs on a single machine. Every request — opening a page, posting a photo — travels from a user’s device to that server and back.\n\nWith a hundred users it barely notices: the CPU idles around **1%**.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "A server has a fixed budget of CPU, memory, disk and network. Its **capacity** is how many requests per second it can serve before one of those runs out."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "100",
        "rps": "4",
        "p95": "80 ms",
        "errors": "0%",
        "cost": "$150"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Viral spike",
      "title": "Then you go viral",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "A post takes off and traffic jumps **120×**. This machine can serve about 400 requests per second, and 480 are arriving. The CPU pins at 100%, requests queue up waiting their turn, latency balloons, and some give up and time out.\n\nYou need more capacity. There are two fundamentally different ways to get it.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Overload looks like: CPU near 100%, growing request queues, rising latency, then timeouts."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Users 100 → 12,000",
        "~ CPU 1% → 100%",
        "+ Requests timing out"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "12,000",
        "rps": "480",
        "p95": "2.4 s",
        "errors": "17%",
        "cost": "$150"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Scale up",
      "title": "Way 1 — scale up",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "The simplest fix: move to a bigger machine. More cores, more memory, faster disks.\n\nNothing about your design changes — the code doesn’t even know. This is [[vertical scaling]], or *scaling up*.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Scaling up is a great first move: zero code changes and nothing new to operate. Four times the machine, roughly four times the price."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Server 4 vCPU → 16 vCPU",
        "~ CPU 100% → 30%",
        "~ Cost $150 → $600 / month"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "12,000",
        "rps": "480",
        "p95": "110 ms",
        "errors": "0%",
        "cost": "$600"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "The ceiling",
      "title": "…until you hit the ceiling",
      "chapter": "tradeoff",
      "body": "Growth continues and you upgrade again and again — but machines only get so big. Even the largest general-purpose server can’t keep up, and software rarely uses 192 cores as well as it used 16: locks, memory and one shared disk get in the way.\n\nEvery upgrade is also a migration: a restart, and downtime while you move.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Vertical scaling has a hard ceiling. In the cloud, price grows roughly in line with size — until the top of the range, where the exotic machines carry a premium and twice the cores no longer means twice the throughput."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Users 12,000 → 500,000",
        "+ Largest machine reached",
        "~ Cost $600 → $7,200 / month"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "500,000",
        "rps": "20,000",
        "p95": "3.1 s",
        "errors": "12%",
        "cost": "$7,200"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "One failure",
      "title": "…and it’s a single point of failure",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Worse, everything depends on one machine. A bad deploy, a full disk or a dead power supply and the whole product is offline.\n\nA component whose failure takes the entire system down is a [[single point of failure]] (SPOF).",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "However big it is, one box is always one failure away from a full outage."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- App server down",
        "~ Errors 12% → 100%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "500,000",
        "rps": "20,000",
        "errors": "100%",
        "cost": "$7,200"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Scale out",
      "title": "Way 2 — scale out",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Instead of one giant machine, run several mid-size ones side by side — here four with 96 vCPU each — all running the same code.\n\nNeed more capacity? Add another server. Mid-size machines are the best value, there’s no hard ceiling, and each one is a small slice of the whole. This is [[horizontal scaling]], or *scaling out* — how virtually every large system grows.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Four 96-vCPU servers have twice the capacity of the biggest single machine — and any one of them can fail while the other three carry the load."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ 1 giant server → 4 mid-size servers",
        "~ Capacity × 2 · cost $7,200 → $14,400 / month",
        "~ CPU ≈ 52% each",
        "~ Errors 100% → 0%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "500,000",
        "rps": "20,000",
        "p95": "95 ms",
        "errors": "0%",
        "cost": "$14,400"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Night time",
      "title": "Peak capacity, idle all night",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Traffic follows the clock. At 8 p.m. you need all four servers; at 4 a.m. traffic is a tenth of that, and they sit at 5% CPU.\n\nYou’re paying for peak capacity 24 hours a day. Most of the time, most of it does nothing.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Consumer apps commonly swing 3–10× between their daily peak and trough."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ 4 a.m.: users 500,000 → 50,000",
        "~ CPU ≈ 52% → 5%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "50,000",
        "rps": "2,000",
        "p95": "90 ms",
        "errors": "0%",
        "cost": "$14,400"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "a-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "b": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "c": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "d": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ua-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ub": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "uc": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ud": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Autoscale",
      "title": "Autoscaling: follow the demand",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Because the servers are interchangeable, an [[autoscaler]] can add and remove them automatically — for example, keep average CPU near 50%.\n\nAt night it scales **in** to two servers — never fewer, so one can still fail — and in the evening it scales back **out**. You pay roughly for what you use.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Autoscaling only works when servers are stateless and sit behind a load balancer — the next two lessons."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- 2 servers removed overnight",
        "~ CPU 5% → 10% on the two that remain",
        "~ Average cost $14,400 → $10,800 / month"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "50,000",
        "rps": "2,000",
        "p95": "90 ms",
        "errors": "0%",
        "cost": "$10,800"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "a-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "b": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "c": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "d": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ua-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ub": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "uc": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ud": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Flash sale",
      "title": "Case: the flash sale",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "At noon two servers are comfortably serving 150,000 users. Then a flash sale starts and traffic jumps **5×** in two minutes. The autoscaler reacts — but a new server takes about three minutes to boot and warm up.\n\nFor those minutes the two running servers can serve only about two-thirds of the traffic. The fix is to scale **before** the spike: scheduled scaling for known events, and spare headroom for unknown ones.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Autoscaling reacts; it can’t predict. Pre-warm for launches, sales and big broadcasts."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Users × 5 at noon",
        "+ 4 servers booting (≈ 3 min)",
        "- Errors while they warm up"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "750,000",
        "rps": "30,000",
        "p95": "2.8 s",
        "errors": "36%",
        "cost": "$21,600"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "a-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "b": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "c": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "d": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ua-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ub": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "uc": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "ud": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "short": "Who routes?",
      "title": "But who sends users where?",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Right now each group of users is hard-wired to one server’s address. When Server B dies, a quarter of your users get errors — even though three healthy servers are right there.\n\nClients can’t tell which servers are alive or busy. Something has to sit in front and steer every request to a healthy server.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "That something is a **load balancer** — the subject of the next lesson."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Server B down",
        "~ Errors 0% → 25%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "500,000",
        "rps": "20,000",
        "p95": "95 ms",
        "errors": "25%",
        "cost": "$14,400"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "users-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "a-6": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "b": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "c": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "d": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ua-6": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ub": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "uc": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ud": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s11",
      "short": "Recap",
      "title": "Recap: up vs out",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "body": "• How — Scale up: Bigger machine; Scale out: More machines\n• Code changes — Scale up: None; Scale out: Some1\n• Ceiling — Scale up: Hardware limit; Scale out: Practically none\n• Failure — Scale up: Total outage; Scale out: Partial2\n• To grow — Scale up: Migrate + restart; Scale out: Add a server\n• Cost — Scale up: Premium at the top; Scale out: Linear\n\nScaling out also lets you **autoscale**: add servers for the evening peak, remove them at night.\n\n1 Servers must be interchangeable — see *Stateless Services*. 2 Zero, once a load balancer routes around failures.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Rule of thumb: scale up while it’s cheap and simple, then scale out."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "users": "500,000",
        "rps": "20,000",
        "p95": "95 ms",
        "errors": "0%",
        "cost": "$14,400"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "b": {
